<div class="path-head"><p class="featured-region">Reading path · 9 stops</p><h1 class="path-title">Al-Andalus</h1><p class="path-tagline">Nearly eight centuries of Muslim Iberia, from Tariq's crossing in 711 to the surrender of Granada in 1492.</p><p class="path-intro">In 711 an army from North Africa crossed into Spain, and for nearly eight centuries much of the Iberian Peninsula was al-Andalus. Under the Umayyads of Córdoba it held the largest city of western Europe and a centre of learning, where Muslims, Christians and Jews lived under one rule, though not as equals. Then it broke into rival kingdoms, was taken over by Berber empires from Morocco and was conquered piece by piece by the Christian kingdoms of the north. This path follows that story through its rulers, buildings and thinkers to the last Muslim state, at Granada.</p><p class="path-types">Suits <a href="/start">the Thinker</a> · <a href="/start">the Timekeeper</a></p></div>
<ol class="path-steps"><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-conquest-of-hispania"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/umayyad-conquest-of-hispania.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">1 · Encyclopedia · Event · 711 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Umayyad conquest of Hispania</span><span class="path-step-why">In 711 Tariq ibn Ziyad crossed from North Africa and defeated King Roderic; within a few years most of the peninsula was under Umayyad rule.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/emirate-of-cordoba"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mosque-cathedral-of-cordoba.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">2 · Profile · 756 CE – 929 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Emirate of Córdoba</span><span class="path-step-why">Founded in 756 by Abd al-Rahman I, a survivor of the Abbasid massacre of his family, it made al-Andalus an independent Islamic state.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/mosque-cathedral-of-cordoba"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mosque-cathedral-of-cordoba.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">3 · Encyclopedia · Work · 785</span><span class="path-step-name">Mosque–Cathedral of Córdoba</span><span class="path-step-why">Begun in 785 and enlarged by later emirs and caliphs, its forest of double-arched columns became a cathedral after the Christian conquest of 1236.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/mozarabs"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mozarabs.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">4 · Encyclopedia · People</span><span class="path-step-name">Mozarabs</span><span class="path-step-why">Christians who lived under Muslim rule and adopted Arabic language and culture while keeping their faith and their own liturgy.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/caliphate-of-cordoba"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-iii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">5 · Profile · 929 CE – 1031 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Caliphate of Córdoba</span><span class="path-step-why">Abd al-Rahman III's caliphate of 929 made Córdoba the largest city of western Europe, with the palace city of Madinat al-Zahra and celebrated libraries.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/taifa"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/taifa.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">6 · Encyclopedia · Institution</span><span class="path-step-name">Taifa</span><span class="path-step-why">When the caliphate broke apart, rival taifa kings paid tribute to Christian rulers, until the fall of Toledo in 1085 brought in the Almoravids.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/averroes"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/averroes.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">7 · Encyclopedia · Person · 1126–1198</span><span class="path-step-name">Averroes</span><span class="path-step-why">Philosopher, physician and judge to the Almohad caliphs, whose commentaries on Aristotle, translated into Latin, shaped European scholasticism.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/maimonides"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maimonides.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">8 · Encyclopedia · Person · c. 1138–1204</span><span class="path-step-name">Maimonides</span><span class="path-step-why">Born in Córdoba, he left al-Andalus with his family under Almohad pressure, settled in Egypt and became one of medieval Judaism's most influential thinkers.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/emirate-of-granada"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alhambra.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">9 · Profile · 1232 CE – 1492 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Emirate of Granada</span><span class="path-step-why">The last Muslim state in Iberia survived as a tributary of Castile for over 250 years, built the Alhambra and surrendered on 2 January 1492.</span></span></a></li></ol>
